# 5. Hermes should NOT own the canonical job queue

This is one place I'd modify our earlier design.

Hermes has a genuinely strong cron system. It supports one-shot and repeating jobs, skill attachment, script-backed jobs, isolated fresh sessions, run history and a gateway scheduler. ([Hermes Agent][2])

But Postiz already brings Temporal into our deployment.

I'd split responsibility like this:

### Temporal

Use for **production state**:

```text
publish this video at 09:00

retry TikTok if publication fails

collect this video's:
T+1h
T+6h
T+24h
T+7d
metrics

wait for human approval

resume rendering after server restart
```

### Hermes cron

Use for **autonomous cognitive routines**:

```text
Every morning:
analyse yesterday's network performance.

Every 6 hours:
look for unusual analytics.

Every night:
review failed agent tasks.

Every Monday:
produce a network strategy report.

Every 12 hours:
search for new story ideas.

Every day:
review comments for emerging audience interests.
```

Hermes cron runs jobs in fresh agent sessions and can inject selected skills into those sessions; it can also execute script-only jobs when no model reasoning is needed. ([Hermes Agent][2])

That's ideal.

### Rule

> **One scheduler owns each timer.**

Never have both Temporal and Hermes cron responsible for publishing the same post.

